## Pregnancy evidence brief: lamotrigine

**Identity:** Lamotrigine was matched exactly in RxNorm. The evidence below concerns the ingredient, not combination products. [S26](https://rxnav.nlm.nih.gov/REST/rxcui/28439/properties.json)

### Summary

Human pregnancy evidence is largely observational: pregnancy registries, epidemiologic studies, reviews, and a practice guideline. In registry data summarized in the selected product label, major congenital-malformation rates after first-trimester **lamotrigine monotherapy** were **2.0%–2.9%** across three registries. The label describes these rates as similar to general-population estimates, while noting that differences in outcome definitions, ascertainment, and comparator groups limit interpretation. [S10](https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=474fb321-575c-4b97-9dbe-080061652ee4)

One NAAED registry analysis reported an oral-cleft signal, but this was not observed in other large international registries; a European case-control analysis did not show a statistically clear association. This remains an evidence inconsistency, not a definitive safety conclusion. [S10](https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=474fb321-575c-4b97-9dbe-080061652ee4)

### 1. Effects on the pregnant person

- Pregnancy reviews describe the evidence in the context of balancing antiseizure-treatment effects with the risks of uncontrolled underlying epilepsy. The retrieved populations were pregnant people with epilepsy, or mixed pregnancy/lactation populations; these findings should not be generalized to every indication for lamotrigine. [S19](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32768086/) [S23](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37684052/)
- The selected label states that pregnancy-related physiological changes may alter lamotrigine concentrations and therapeutic effect. This is source clinical-consideration text, not an individualized treatment plan. [S10](https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=474fb321-575c-4b97-9dbe-080061652ee4)
- A 2024 AAN/AES/SMFM practice guideline reviewed antiseizure-medication exposure and fetal outcomes in people with epilepsy of childbearing potential; its population and recommendations are specific to that clinical context. [S24](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38748979/)

### 2. Measured fetal or infant exposure

- No clear quantified fetal-concentration estimate for lamotrigine was retrieved in this brief.
- A naturalistic study of **60 mothers** examined psychotropic-drug concentrations during pregnancy and lactation and followed exposed children during the first 12 months. Its source population was mixed—pregnancy, lactation, neonates, and children—and the abstract does not provide a lamotrigine-specific sample size or a fetal-exposure measurement. [S22](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36103361/)
- The abstract reported lamotrigine concentration findings in breast milk; that is lactation exposure evidence, not fetal-exposure evidence. [S22](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36103361/)

### 3. Observed fetal or infant outcomes

**Major congenital malformations after first-trimester monotherapy exposure:**

- International Lamotrigine Pregnancy Registry: **2.2%** among 1,558 exposed infants; 95% CI 1.6%–3.1%.
- NAAED Pregnancy Registry: **2.0%** among 1,562 exposed infants.
- EURAP: **2.9%** among 2,514 exposures; 95% CI 2.3%–3.7%.

These are registry observations summarized in the product label, not randomized-trial results. [S10](https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=474fb321-575c-4b97-9dbe-080061652ee4)

**Oral clefts:**

- The NAAED registry reported **3.2 per 1,000** among 2,200 infants exposed early in pregnancy, described in the label as a threefold increase versus unexposed healthy controls.
- The label states that this finding was not observed in other large international pregnancy registries.
- A European case-control study reported an adjusted odds ratio of **1.45**, with a 95% CI of **0.8–2.63**. [S10](https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=474fb321-575c-4b97-9dbe-080061652ee4)

**Other outcomes:**

The label states that meta-analyses evaluated fetal death, stillbirth, preterm birth, small-for-gestational-age birth, and neurodevelopmental delay. It reports no data suggesting increased risk with lamotrigine monotherapy, but says methodological differences limit conclusions. This does not establish that these outcomes cannot occur. [S10](https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=474fb321-575c-4b97-9dbe-080061652ee4)

The 60-mother naturalistic study reported no significant differences in birth outcomes or first-year development between its in-utero-exposed and nonexposed groups, but the retrieved abstract does not establish a lamotrigine-specific estimate or fully characterize the comparison groups. [S22](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36103361/)

### Evidence limitations

- The PubMed pregnancy search returned 690 records, but only a bounded relevance sample and abstracts were retrieved; it was not a systematic review. [S11](https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=pubmed&term=%28%28%22lamotrigine%22%5BMeSH+Terms%5D+OR+%22lamotrigine%22%5BSubstance+Name%5D+OR+%22lamotrigine%22%5BTitle%2FAbstract%5D%29%29+AND+%28%22Pregnancy%22%5BMeSH+Terms%5D+OR+pregnan%2A%5BTitle%2FAbstract%5D+OR+gestation%2A%5BTitle%2FAbstract%5D%29&retmax=5&sort=relevance&retmode=json)
- The pharmacokinetic search returned 109 records, also with a bounded sample rather than a systematic review. [S12](https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=pubmed&term=%28%28%22lamotrigine%22%5BMeSH+Terms%5D+OR+%22lamotrigine%22%5BSubstance+Name%5D+OR+%22lamotrigine%22%5BTitle%2FAbstract%5D%29%29+AND+%28%22Pregnancy%22%5BMeSH+Terms%5D+OR+pregnan%2A%5BTitle%2FAbstract%5D+OR+gestation%2A%5BTitle%2FAbstract%5D%29+AND+%28%22Pharmacokinetics%22%5BMeSH+Terms%5D+OR+%22Drug+Elimination+Routes%22%5BMeSH+Terms%5D+OR+pharmacokinetic%2A%5BTitle%2FAbstract%5D+OR+clearance%5BTitle%2FAbstract%5D+OR+%22area+under+the+curve%22%5BTitle%2FAbstract%5D+OR+%22dose+adjustment%22%5BTitle%2FAbstract%5D%29&retmax=3&sort=relevance&retmode=json)
- The selected DailyMed record is **LAMOTRIGINE KIT (LAMOTRIGINE) KIT LAMOTRIGINE TABLET**, OWP Pharmaceuticals, Inc., effective October 12, 2026. It is one product label, not all formulations. The composite label pregnancy section was truncated, although parsed pregnancy subsections were available. [S10](https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=474fb321-575c-4b97-9dbe-080061652ee4)
- Much of the pregnancy evidence concerns monotherapy and people with epilepsy. Applicability to polytherapy, bipolar disorder, other indications, or a different formulation is not established by this brief.
- FAERS reporting counts are reporting volume, not incidence, risk, or causation; the query has no exposure denominator and is not a complete exposed-pregnancy population. [S2](https://api.fda.gov/drug/event.json?search=patient.drug.openfda.generic_name%3A%22lamotrigine%22+AND+patient.reaction.reactionmeddrapt%3A%22foetal+exposure+during+pregnancy%22&count=patient.reaction.reactionmeddrapt.exact&limit=8) [S13](https://open.fda.gov/apis/drug/event/)
- Missing evidence is not evidence of safety. This generated summary is not clinically validated.

### Participation information

- The FDA directory lists the North American Antiepileptic Drug in Pregnancy Registry for lamotrigine/Lamictal. Directory status is not an eligibility determination or confirmation of current enrollment. [S28](https://www.fda.gov/consumers/pregnancy-exposure-registries/list-pregnancy-exposure-registries) [S27](https://www.aedpregnancyregistry.org/join-our-study/)
- **NCT05450978 — RECRUITING:** an observational study of medication clearance and exposure during pregnancy and breastfeeding in **pregnant adults with epilepsy**; the record lists lamotrigine among the medications studied and the University of Pittsburgh as a recruiting location. Eligibility and site availability were not confirmed by this lookup. [S9](https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05450978) [S3](https://clinicaltrials.gov/api/v2/studies?query.term=lamotrigine+AND+%28pregnancy%29&filter.overallStatus=RECRUITING&pageSize=20&format=json)

